"""r17's check texts in the romance article (2026-11-07), each linted before its check (gate bad-trips-2026-11-07h).
- "Situationships" after its fix (his answer 28a): paragraph 34 as it read Human with no Low by itself (#95), and 36's
  closing line (Low in the live post and in #86) said once, in its opening sentence, where "much deeper" already said it
  wasn't superficial (E378: a repeat cut).
- Three sections with a fixed spot and open Low windows, each whole with the spots' best tries in place, heading left
  out: "Some babies grow up faster" (231 to 241, S11 as #37 and S12 as #13 read; 238's short closing joined with but),
  "Different levels in different domains can be complementary" (243 to 250, S13 as #14 read; 250 with its advice said
  first, "lest" said plainly and the closing aside put in brackets) and "What are you actually choosing together?" (341 to 347, S20 as #27 read; 342's and
  344's short sentences joined to the ones before them). "Two Pillars Don't Hold The Roof Up" waits: its Low window is on
  the paragraph the owner lock keeps as the live post has it.
- Two sections of the post's last part with Low windows (#47): "Why all of this sounds artificial" (404 to 413; 410's
  short opener joined to what it says with a colon) and "Gaslighting in the dark" (431 to 435; 433's closing guideline
  said as what friends should do, 435's two short sentences joined with but). "What I gained from loss" goes in the next
  round: 443's short line is his opening's point."""
TEXTS = {
 "r17-sit": "And then there’s the situationship, which is hard to define because that’s basically the point. Usually it’s a happenstance relationship based on sex. There’s less communication, and what communication exists is more superficial. Yet it is obviously some kind of relationship even while one or both people refuse to name it. You’re regularly having sex, going out together, spending time alone together, and doing things you wouldn’t do with a regular friend, so it feels like it’s filling a relationship void, but only accidentally and occasionally—maybe just enough to keep you from looking for anything deeper.\n\nIt can go either way, but often the man wants to keep it undefined while the woman wants commitment, partly because she’s the one risking pregnancy. Whoever keeps it undefined gets the fun without (most of) the responsibility. But people still get pregnant and attached, whatever they call it. The one keeping it that way still ends up with the existential void another superficial fix creates. Doomscrolling entertains you too.\n\nAnd “Why are you making this heavy?” can be a real question. They may actually mean: don’t you want the fun, and are you about to give it up? My answer would be: I’m trying to understand whether this is really fulfilling me or if it’s drainingly superficial.\n\nI had something sort of like that with H., although ours was much deeper than what people normally mean by situationship, and painfully uneven. We were (and still are) BFFs, and spent nearly all our time together. She was falling in love with me, and I wasn’t falling in love with her. She felt more like a sister to me, partly because I’m not attracted to white women.",
 "r17-babies": "While different maturity levels are to be expected to some degree, it can get out of hand at the extremes. What I noticed in all of my most intimate relationships was that when one person was always the child and the other is always the therapist, guru, doctor, or dad, then it’s too unequal, and eventually there will be some kind of pendulum swing or backlash. When I became consistently the responsible one, that also made me responsible for everything that went wrong, not to mention the unhealthy clinginess, jealousy, and all the rest which went along with that dynamic.\n\nToft says, “She’s not a girl,” and “She’s not your mother.” I agree with the warning, but I’d qualify the first sentence. A lot of women enjoy a daddy/little-girl dimension with a man they trust, and I don’t see a reason to deny them that. It can be affectionate, erotic, comforting, or playful.\n\nThe problem is when the role takes over the relationship. She can be my little girl sometimes without becoming my child. She still has to remain responsible for her life and keep reparenting herself.\n\nIt can run the other way too. If she has to calm me down every time I’m upset, and on top of that organize my life and keep me functional, then I’ve made her Mom.\n\nAll three women told me at some point that they felt like my patient, and I couldn’t exactly argue with them. They asked me about almost every medical, mental-health, and practical problem:\n\n“I’m sick. What should I take?”\n\n“I’m sad. What should I do?”\n\nI usually had some idea, so of course I answered, but enough moments become a pattern.\n\nKey at first asked me innocently, “Can you be my guru?” I told her, “I’m not anyone’s guru, but we can learn from each other.” A few months later, she was telling me she was much more spiritually advanced than me. I asked, “Do you recall asking me to be your guru?” She said, “Wow, I must have been really confused back then!”\n\nI also have to admit, I can become condescending when somebody stops taking responsibility for their behavior. I find that intolerable. If someone tells me, “I feel like your patient,” we both have to look at what we’re doing.\n\nI know what happens on my side. Helping feels good, and so does being needed. A lot of the time I really did have the answer or the perspective they needed, so of course they kept asking me. I also had to look at whether they were trying to take care of themselves without me.",
 "r17-levels": "According to myself, I was ahead of Bee, Key, and H. in maturity, emotional stability, self-love, and self-awareness. I also had advantages they didn’t, including a happier childhood and more moolah. As you might imagine, all of that affected the balance between us.\n\nThey were all ahead of me in childlike wonder and fascinating creative intuition, which is what attracted me. Sometimes their intuition was wrong and they trusted it too much, but I still loved that quality in them, since I tend to be more analytical.\n\nThey were also all more social than me. They could organize people for a ceremony or group gathering very easily. I felt like I needed that if I ever wanted to start a commune, because I’m more introverted.\n\nI’m good at talking to people when there’s a purpose. I’m not as good at rousing everybody up and encouraging them to do things or go places with me, because I tend to feel that everyone should simply do as they please.\n\nSo I do believe in mutual coaching and complementarity.\n\nI could teach these women about loving and reparenting their inner child, Buddhism, meditation, and other things I understood better. They could pull me into life more, show me where I was missing people, and sometimes be the skeptic when I was seeing the best in someone and missing their hidden motives.\n\nWhen it works, we take turns, me teaching one day and learning the next, and that’s healthy.\n\nIt’s time to bring in other support once a partner has become the default caregiver and that’s taken for granted, or the romance could collapse under the burden. Maybe the romance actually does need less intensity, maybe it becomes friendship, or maybe it ends. Asking the question out loud is usually where it starts, though I can’t answer it for others (and couldn’t always answer it for myself).",
 "r17-choosing": "A lot of romantic pain starts with two lovey bunnies entering one of the biggest arrangements of their lives without ever discussing their ideas, dreams, and fears about it.\n\n“We’re together” ... what does that mean exactly? I can hear a whole future in those two words—sex, exclusivity, living together, money, children, caregiving. She might mean, “We really like each other; let’s see what happens.” I actually like letting a relationship flow with some parts left unnamed, but the problem is when we’re using the same words for two different futures.\n\nSometimes what two people want is much more practical. They want to share a home, raise children, support each other financially, take care of each other, maybe have sex, and not pretend all of that depends on overwhelming romantic love. I’d call that a practical partnership. There’s nothing inherently wrong with that arrangement if both people know that’s what they’re doing. But I suspect they simply have never been in love, so they don’t have that expectation. And that becomes a problem if they ever do fall for someone.\n\nAnother honest option is a romance with humility, even if romance and humility seem to clash. Two people really are in love and want to build something together, and they can choose exclusivity, shack up, have children, and plan a life, as long as nobody pretends they can guarantee what either heart will feel ten years later.\n\nThen the questions change for me. If the romance changes, which parts are still supposed to remain? Are we promising actions, or low-key pretending to promise permanent feelings? Which needs are we still going to meet outside the relationship?\n\nAnd the biggie: what happens if one of us falls in love with someone else? What counts as betrayal, and what’s just a sign we need a serious talk? If our lives change, can we honestly sit down and rethink the agreement? If we split up, what do we still owe each other, and what do we owe our kids, if we have any?\n\nThese are just a few questions I could think of to start the ball rolling, but be creative and see what you come up with! You can make a card game out of this and have some fun with it. If there’s real love, it will survive a bit of honest curiosity. And again, community can make all of this a lot less scary.",
 "r17-artificial": "By now this probably sounds terribly artificial. Is anyone actually going to do scheduled check-ins, delayed cohabitation, conversations about hypothetical disasters, imagination exercises, not to mention resisting the heat-of-the-moment stuff and continuing your own reparenting practices when a warm body is available to do some of the soothing?\n\nWell, yeah. It is painfully artificial. In a healthy communal society, the couple would have access to witnesses, elders, friends, and other emotional outlets and resources. Most of us these days don’t have that, so we’re trying to recreate consciously and clumsily some of what the wider field would have done without a checklist.\n\nMost of this article is about what to do before jumping head first into head over shoulders. But if you’re already married with children, advice about slowing down arrives a little late.\n\nI wish I had a special method for fixing that. Mostly I learned the obvious thing the hard way: one person can’t fix the relationship (unless they’re the main problem in it).\n\nWith Bee and Key, the biggest thing sitting underneath everything was trust. They wanted to be with me and would say they trusted me, but really they didn’t trust me, themself, or anyone else. It was all or nothing: blind trust, or none. I kept saying, “It’s not possible to have a relationship without trust.” I think that rigidity came from trauma and never really growing up.\n\nEven dishonesty was sometimes hard for me to define in these relationships. Bee once opened a door and, a second later, said she hadn’t opened it. That drove me crazy. Why would she lie like that? She was lying, but “she lied” only named the momentary behavior. There was no strategy behind it, and she was usually quite honest. Looking back, I could have understood the lie in the context of some kind of mental illness instead of treating it like strategic dishonesty.\n\nSome things were less mysterious: they were acting as if the trust problem wasn’t there because they still wanted the relationship. Attractions to other people sometimes went unsaid too, and sometimes got imagined from nowhere. Some people keep a relationship like this going for a long time, just sweeping everything under the rug.\n\nOn the other hand, sometimes I was more honest than my partner could handle, which was its own kind of damage. Ask whether both of you actually want to prioritize radical honesty, first. But the other person also can’t keep saying “not now” forever and think that’s going to save the relationship.\n\nThis is the time for agape to shine, because the best thing for both of you may not be holding onto the relationship pattern you originally committed to or dreamt of.\n\nWhen did you two last dance? And not the “we dance around our problems” joke (LOL).. If that’s where things are at, the trust is out the window. Ouch. I know that one from experience with my first 2 wives and K, too. That generally marks the point of no return, so try not to wait for that. Because once you’re there, as my dad explained a thousand times whenever we had a visitor, unconscious resentment begins to snowball, and we feel colder together than alone. Pretty soon, old Romeo & Juliette might get wandering eye syndrome.",
 "r17-gaslight": "Do you have irreconcilable memories of key events? I did with both my first and second wives. I took my first wife, Ann, to two counselors. The male counselor eventually told us, “I’ve never told anyone this, but you guys need to split up for at least six months.”\n\nThere’s no way to resolve a situation where one person remembers that you ruined her life on purpose, while you remember that she was having a manic episode and had to be committed for her own safety. We also didn’t have mutual friends who could corroborate what happened—only friends on each side who were partial.\n\nMany say it’s best to save face and work things out privately, but I’d disagree. If you don’t have friends who can help with the conflict, then you’re really stuck (and you might wanna find some new friends). But do it honestly, with the goal of helping the conflict, not just getting approval for your own interpretation. Both people should get a chance to speak, and good friends should hear both sides before validating either.\n\nI can believe a friend’s experience and still tell them that my ability to know what is really going on is extremely limited. If someone says, “I don’t have all the evidence,” I trust their thinking more. Living in community would help because your friends would not only hear two stories afterward. Sometimes they would actually see how the two of you treat each other when you argue.\n\nIf you’ve been there for someone, don’t abandon them in a crisis unless it’s unsafe for you to stay or they’re creating the crisis to keep you, or they aren’t willing to help themself. You may need to arrange other care, but that’s no reason to pretend the romance still has a future."
}
